{
  "dom_id": "step-q210542",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The electrostatic force $F,$ in newtons ($N$), exerted between two identical charged particles kept at a fixed distance apart, varies directly with the **square** of the charge $q$ of each particle, measured in coulombs $(C).$ If the electrostatic force is $8\\,976\\,N$ when the charges are $2\\,C,$ then express $F$ in terms of $q.$\n\n$F=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The electrostatic force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the charge $q,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF=k{q}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $F=8\\,976\\,\\text{N}$ when $q=2\\,\\text{C}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\,976 & =k\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 8\\,976 & =k\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{8\\,976}{4} & =k \\\\ 2\\,244 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nF=2\\,244{q}^{2}.\n$$",
